  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1219 AM CST Thu Nov 19 2026\n\nA mid-level ridge continues to have heavy influence on the weather \npattern for South Texas, but there is still a surface low in the \nnorthern Gulf. This will allow for light northeasterly flow of winds \nfor the region to remain and pull in that cooler air. Winds will \nshift to the east heading into the weekend, preventing any \nsignificant chances for moisture as the PWAT values for the CWA stay \nbelow the typical average of November. Temperatures remain seasonal \nwith highs in the mid to upper 70s and lows ranging from the mid 50s \nto low 60s closer to the coast for the rest of the week and into the \nbeginning of the weekend. \n\nThe subtropical jet begins to ramp up this weekend just as a deep \ntrough is working its way across the Western US. By the time the \ntrough reaches the Great Plains is when the subtropical jet \nstrengthens, advecting upper-level moisture into the South Texas \nregion, additionally with the onshore flow at the surface. GEFS is \nshowing PWAT values expected to increase about 1.3-1.5 inches, which \nis above normal for this time of the year. Low chance (20-30%) for \nshowers and thunderstorms Sunday ahead of the cold front. Confidence \nis low for rain chances early next week as the front likely dries \nthings out for the region. Temperatures will remain relatively \ncooler starting early next week and possibly extending to \nThanksgiving. \n\nMinor coastal flooding continues today and possibly into Friday due \nto northeasterly flow and Eckman circulation pushing water into the \ncoast.",
